diff --git a/Documentation/kbuild/reproducible-builds.rst b/Documentation/kbuild/reproducible-builds.rst
index b150196..2e4f354 100644
--- a/Documentation/kbuild/reproducible-builds.rst
+++ b/Documentation/kbuild/reproducible-builds.rst
@@ -84,8 +84,49 @@ generate a different temporary key for each build, resulting in the
 modules being unreproducible.  However, including a signing key with
 your source would presumably defeat the purpose of signing modules.
 
-Instead ``CONFIG_MODULE_HASHES`` can be used to embed a static list
-of valid modules to load.
+Instead ``CONFIG_MODULE_HASHES`` can be used to authenticate a reproducible
+set of modules without a signing key. The kernel contains a fixed-size
+``.module_hashes`` section. Packaging fills this section after the final
+in-tree and out-of-tree module closure is known.
+
+``scripts/modules-merkle-tree`` takes a file containing one path to a final,
+uncompressed ``.ko`` per line and a path for its binary root output. The list
+order is part of the format and must be deterministic. For every listed
+module, the tool writes a proof beside it with ``.merkle`` appended to its
+name. Kbuild provides an explicit late-finalization target that runs the tool
+and stamps both ``vmlinux.unstripped`` and ``vmlinux``::
+
+  make O=build MODULE_HASH_LIST="$PWD/modules.list" module-hashes
+
+Packaging then appends each proof and rebuilds any architecture-specific
+image that contains ``vmlinux``::
+
+  while IFS= read -r module; do
+          cat "$module.merkle" >> "$module"
+  done < modules.list
+  make O=build bzImage
+
+The host tool can also be called directly when a packaging system wants to
+manage the ``objcopy --update-section`` step itself::
+
+  build/scripts/modules-merkle-tree modules.list module-hashes.root
+
+Strip and sign modules before generating the tree. Compress modules and sign
+or wrap the kernel image after stamping it. This ordering lets packaging add
+out-of-tree modules without rebuilding or relinking the kernel.
+
+The version 1 root is a 44-byte, target-endian-independent format:
+
+* bytes 0--3: ASCII ``KMHT``;
+* byte 4: format version, ``1``;
+* byte 5: hash algorithm, ``1`` for SHA-256;
+* byte 6: tree algorithm, ``1`` for the binary tree described by the tool;
+* byte 7: zero, reserved;
+* bytes 8--11: tree levels as a big-endian 32-bit integer;
+* bytes 12--43: the SHA-256 Merkle root.
+
+An unstamped kernel contains a zero-level root and rejects Merkle-authenticated
+modules.
 
 Another approach to this is to divide up the build process so that the
 unreproducible parts can be treated as sources:
